<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Durance is a river of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 323 km long, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> near Briançon and flowing south-west through Provence to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. Its valley has long been a route between the Alps and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and in antiquity it bounded the lands of Gallic peoples such as the Cavares. Today it supplies much of Provence with water and hydroelectric power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durance.jpg" alt="Durance" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathieu BROSSAIS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_depuis_la_chapelle_Notre-Dame-de-Beauregard_d%27Orgon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
